3df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/assets/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355754 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1089 "q-12040" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1100 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1113 #uuid "e0248467-d4d0-53ed-a76a-c05070c65759" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355753 1145 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=5.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $5.$" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355755 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355756 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355757 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355758 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355759 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1041 "selection" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355755 13194139535574 true] [17592186355754 1110 #uuid "abf5e327-2fbf-5dd2-90a1-99f583250954" 13194139535574 true] [17592186355755 1034 "Dilation with sca